  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nStratocumulus deck has been stubborn to leave the southeast CWA \nearly this afternoon, but HRRR sounding trends suggest gradual \nscattering out of these clouds into late afternoon. Increased sky \ncover grids for a few more hours in this area to reflect \"mostly \ncloudy\" wording until later in the afternoon.\n\nWill continue to watch potential for strong/severe storms this \nafternoon across ND into far northern SD. CAMs continue to be rather \nscant in overall coverage, and will also be dealing with capping \nissues this far south. MLCAPE values increase to around 2000 J/KG \nalong the surface boundary later this afternoon. DCAPE values rather \nsporty from around 1400-1800 J/KG. So, any convection that manages \nto fire and make it into the northern CWA is capable of gusty winds \nperhaps over 60 mph. PoPs generally reflect 20-30% across the \nnorthern CWA into the early evening hours.\n\nFocus then shifts to the increasing heat/humidity this weekend as \nwarmer air aloft (+32 to +35C at 850mb) spreads eastward over the \nNorthern Plains. Highs look to soar into the 90s and low 100s, but \nam slightly concerned about smoke and the potential NBM high bias \ncausing actual highs to be a couple degrees shy of forecast. \nNonetheless, it would still appear much of the CWA comes close, or \ninto low-end advisory criteria Saturday afternoon, as well as \nSunday. Went ahead and collaboration a 2-day Heat Advisory covering \nboth Saturday and Sunday's expected heat indices from 95 to 105 \nacross much of the CWA.\n\nSpeaking of smoke from earlier in the discussion, latest run of HRRR-\nsmoke does bring fairly high concentrations of smoke aloft tonight \nand into the day Saturday. Whether or not it's enough to hold \ntemperatures back a few degrees is yet to be seen. Latest visible \nimagery shows appreciable thickness across southern Canada where \ncurrent HRRR-smoke output suggests around 100-175 mg/m^2. \nConcentrations this high in the HRRR-smoke are generally confined to \nND and northern MN tonight through Saturday, so tough to say if we \nsee thickness enough to keep temps down slightly, which could also \nfactor into reaching heat advisory criteria or not. There are even \nsome indications there could be surface smoke to contend with (low \nconcentrations) over the northern CWA during the day Saturday, so \ninserted/expanded mention of this in the grids/forecast. \n\nIt still appears there is potential (20-30%) for a few storms across \nthe high plains of western SD Saturday afternoon, possibly moving \ninto the western CWA late in the afternoon into early evening. \nSoundings reveal most likely threat would be gusty winds in excess \nof 60 mph. SPC Day 2 outlook still has Marginal Risk over the \nsouthwest CWA to cover this.\n\nLooking ahead further into the forecast, hot temperatures look to \nstick around through much of the period, with inherited NBM highs \nranging from the upper 80s to upper 90s Sunday through Friday of \nnext week. Best opportunity for precipitation (30-50%) looks to be \nWednesday into Wednesday night with a passing system. NBM \nprobability for anything more than 0.50in is only around 20 percent \nor less though.",
